ncentration  category we place our flowers into the  medium category  and then isolates and concentrates will  be described as high concentration  products  and no matter what the matrix is  everything is going to start by getting  homogenized  so this allows us to take a  representative homogenous sample of the  submitted product  and this can be done differently in  laboratories either using a grinder  liquid nitrogen stainless steel  agitators  or cryogrinder  so once the sample has been checked in  homogenized and weighed out based on  whatever test  we are testing for we are then going to  extract it so  even though we had categories where  chocolate and oil end up in the same  concentration category those are  actually going to be extracted  differently  so samples are extracted according to  the specific matrix  and as of right now laboratories are  doing this differently  so far aoac has approved an official  method for cannabinoids  and they're working on many others but  this is the only  official approved method so far  once everything is extracted and ready  to go we are ready for analysis  as i mentioned we analyze for  cannabinoids  terpenes residual solvents heavy metals  pesticides  mycotoxins and microbials and all of  these  are done differently we can have a  sample come in that is solely for  cannabinoid testing or we can have a  sample come in that  needs a full panel  so we'll first look at cannabinoids  in our laboratory we're testing  cannabinoids using high performance  liquid chromatography or hplc  and this can be tested with many  different methods  some laboratories are using gcms some  laboratories are using lcm sms  or other methods in our laboratory we're  looking at 12 main cannabinoids  and other laboratories will look for six  some will look for 16  depends on what the customers are  looking for and are interested in seeing  their products  we also are reporting total tac  in total cbd based on these numbers  and so federally we know that the action  limit for delta 9 thc  based on dry weight is 0.3 percent um  here in vermont vermont has actually set  a one percent limit on total thc  and then if we're looking at moisture  content which plays a role in all of our  analyses  we want our trim flower and this is all  specifically for hemp here  in vermont to be 13  moisture and 0.65 water activity  next is residual solvents residual  solvents are found when  a cannabis product  has been extracted using some type of  solvent  and this needs to be removed before the  customer actually ingests it so we're  testing to see if there's any solvents  that are left over we are using gas  chromatography  mass spec to do this because these  most of these compounds are pretty  volatile  and you can see the state of vermont has  set limits  for a list of residual solvents  that we do not want to see show up in  customer samples over these limits  using gcms we're also testing terpenes  because they're also volatile compounds  we're looking at 22 terpenes to include  limonene  pinene and caryethylene  while this is not one of the regulated  list of compounds by the state of  vermont um  it basically because it is not  associated with potency and is not  considered a contaminant either  we are still looking at these compounds  because customers are interested in  seeing their  terpene profile the importance of the  terpene profile for customers is that  not only does it give the aromatic  signature of that product but it also  has been shown to  contribute to the entourage effect and  have some  medicinal benefits  also looking at heavy metals for this we  are using icpms technology  and we are looking for four main metals  the importance of looking for heavy  metals is that the canvas plant is  actually really good at  uptaking heavy metals from the soil as  well as from any agricultural water used  so the state of vermont has set limits  for  action limits for flour as well as  a list of limits for concentrates  products and infused products  next we can look at pesticides we are  using liquid chromatography  dual mass spectrometry for this and  with this technology we are able to use  look at  60 different pesticides pesticides are  important because they are being used  in the hemp arena and the cannabis arena  just like they are in in most crops  and the state of vermont has a pre  comprehensive list here of  15 different pesticides um but i  you know that other states are actually  looking at you know 90 plus pesticides  so this is a rather short list compared  to those states  we are also looking at microbials  so we have a couple different  methodologies that we use  to look at these when looking at total  yeast in mold  and total aerobic bacteria these are  quantitative  methods used on plates you can use  petrofilm  and these are plate counts essentially  we are also looking at mycotoxins  we use lcm sms to do this  quantitatively and on the lcm sms we can  test for  aflatoxins a1a2 g1g2  as well as opertoxin a  there are also specific bacterias you  can test for  aspergillus which is actually a fungus  those different species can actually be  tested for  via pcr these are qualitative tests  usually  and the state of vermont has set limits  for microbials  we have different limits for trim flower  different limits for concentrates  and different for products and abuse  products  so once everything has been analyzed for  the requested tests  we do a final check on all parameters so  that's making sure that our blanks are  blank  it's making sure that our controls and  check standards  have come in at the correct  concentrations that we're expecting them  to  we want to make sure that our  calibrations are linear  and we also want to make sure that in  our chromatogram is spectra that our  peaks are showing up at the correct  times  and that they are being identified  correctly  if we have to double check we can always  use  nist to crop cross reference for some of  our testings  so once all the results have been  validated and approved  we can then move on to generating  reports and  this comes with a whole list of its own  approval and checklist  but once the report has been generated  and checked  we can then release that certificate of  analysis to the customer  one of the main points here for the  customer is to make sure that there are  explanations  so for example on our reports we have  a small box that gives it gives a  explanation of how total thc is  calculated how total cbd is calculated  how the percent moisture plays a role in  their results  and this is really important because  this is a fairly new industry we have  farmers that are new to the industry  and so sometimes that means a phone call  sometimes that means some supplemental  information for people  and just to make sure that they  understand that what their results mean  for their  product this is really important for  them because  the state of vermont has set limits for  what their results should be compared to  the label value that they're placing on  that product  one really great resource for cannabis  testing workflows  are lens software so limbs are really  great at data management  generating reports and keeping  everything really organized and flowing  smoothly  so there are lens systems out there that  are specific to cannabis laboratories  and will really keep your workflow  efficient and working correctly  so once everything has been generated  and given to the customer  billing and invoicing will be done and  then  we will store the sample for future use  or  disposal  so what are some challenges that we're  facing currently within the cannabis  testing workflow  one of the biggest ones is the lack of  data the lack of validation work done  there are stan few standard methods as i  mentioned  the aoac method for cannabinoids  is one of the only ones out there right  now  and um we are like i mentioned  the different number of matrices 